session.\ndocs/architecture.md:124:- `CodexInvocation` records the command, arguments, and prompt used for an invocation.\ndocs/architecture.md:143:`src/cli.rs::CommandChat<B>` is the command orchestration surface shared by the CLI, controller, and\ndocs/architecture.md:148:- project root and prompt policy,\ndocs/architecture.md:151:- orchestrator, patch, review, and linearizer coordination,\ndocs/architecture.md:152:- transcript output for command-mode and UI consumers,\ndocs/architecture.md:157:- `CommandChat::new` for constructing command chat state with a backend.\ndocs/architecture.md:159:- `CommandChat::handle_line` for processing command lines such as `new`, `chat`, `review`, and\ndocs/architecture.md:160:  `linearize`.\ndocs/architecture.md:167:`CommandChat` uses the active `AgentProfile` when launching user agents, reviewers, and linearizers.\ndocs/architecture.md:179:- LLM-generated chat titles through hidden `title-<agent-id>` backend launches, with\ndocs/architecture.md:180:  `src/chat_title.rs::ChatTitleAgent` tracking first-prompt naming state,\ndocs/architecture.md:181:- command transcripts,\ndocs/architecture.md:182:- background launch/send/review workers,\ndocs/architecture.md:183:- stream routing from `AgentStreamEvent` into the selected session,\ndocs/architecture.md:184:- review startup, automatic per-patch-agent review routing, reviewer-session creation, and\ndocs/architecture.md:185:  reviewed-commit bookkeeping,\ndocs/architecture.md:189:loading state. A user-agent response becomes review-ready only when the orchestrator transcript shows\ndocs/architecture.md:190:an applied patch from that agent and the agent emits `@work-leaf done`. Successful patch application\ndocs/architecture.md:191:returns a continuation prompt to the patch agent when the agent has not reported done, so the agent can\ndocs/architecture.md:192:run repository-required checks through locked command directives, provide follow-up patches, or signal\ndocs/architecture.md:193:review readiness. Repository build, test, format, and required-check commands run only through\ndocs/architecture.md:194:agent-emitted orchestrator directives that name the command and the write-lock paths the command may\ndocs/architecture.md:195:touch. Locked command runs have a five-minute default timeout, after which the command is terminated,\ndocs/architecture.md:197:instruction files into agent prompts, and the active backend agent is responsible for choosing and\ndocs/architecture.md:198:requesting the repository checks required by those instructions before reporting work done.\ndocs/architecture.md:199:Tracked file changes produced by locked commands remain pending for that patch agent until the agent\ndocs/architecture.md:200:commits them through the patch protocol or reverts them. Pending command changes block\ndocs/architecture.md:201:`@work-leaf done`, and the orchestrator returns the tracked diff so the agent can submit the command\ndocs/architecture.md:204:Review bookkeeping has three scopes. The controller records a launch-time review baseline for each\ndocs/architecture.md:205:patch agent, tracks the latest reviewed hash for that patch agent so the same agent head is not\ndocs/architecture.md:206:reviewed twice, and asks reviewers to inspect every provisional commit from the active baseline\ndocs/architecture.md:207:through the latest patch-agent commit. `CommandChat` also keeps the ordered exact review targets that\ndocs/architecture.md:208:completed review during the active instance, and those targets form the linearizer handoff. This lets\ndocs/architecture.md:209:one patch-agent session complete more than one reviewed patch without a later hash replacing earlier\ndocs/architecture.md:210:reviewed work in the linearizer prompt.\ndocs/architecture.md:212:The command transcript is also the conversation history for the persistent `command-agent`. That\ndocs/architecture.md:213:system agent interprets chat sent to the Work Leaf command surface. It recognizes literal command\ndocs/architecture.md:214:lines and common natural-language requests for help, review, linearization, quitting, and launching\ndocs/architecture.md:215:one or more user agents. Multi-agent launch requests dispatch `new [prompt...]` once per requested\ndocs/architecture.md:216:agent through the same controller paths used by command-mode input.\ndocs/architecture.md:223:- `execute_command_line` to run command-mode input.\ndocs/architecture.md:224:- `create_agent` to reserve, select, and launch an agent session from a prompt.\ndocs/architecture.md:225:- `send_command_agent_message` to route chat from the Work Leaf command surface to `command-agent`.\ndocs/architecture.md:226:- `send_message` to send a prompt to one session while other sessions may still be busy.\ndocs/architecture.md:227:- `start_review` to create or resume reviewer sessions for explicit history-wide review and stream\ndocs/architecture.md:228:  reviewer output.\ndocs/architecture.md:234:- `WorkLeafSnapshot`, which contains the command transcript and sessions.\ndocs/architecture.md:243:`CommandTranscriptLine` carries one new command transcript line. `AgentStatusUpdated` carries\ndocs/architecture.md:250:spawning, session naming, review lookup, loading bookkeeping, or orchestrator event routing.\ndocs/architecture.md:260:- `POST /command` calls `WorkLeafController::execute_command_line`.\ndocs/architecture.md:261:- `POST /command-agent` calls `WorkLeafController::send_command_agent_message`.\ndocs/architecture.md:275:The terminal frontend is an adapter over the UI-neutral controller surface.\ndocs/architecture.md:277:`src/terminal_app.rs::TerminalApp<B>` translates raw terminal bytes and modal editing state into\ndocs/architecture.md:278:direct `WorkLeafController<B>` calls for in-process use. `src/terminal_app.rs::RemoteTerminalApp`\ndocs/architecture.md:279:uses the same terminal state machine with `HttpControllerClient` for the CLI/daemon process split.\ndocs/architecture.md:282:frame. They own terminal event-loop concerns such as insert mode, prompt mode, `Ctrl-W` navigation,\ndocs/architecture.md:283:SGR mouse clicks, SGR mouse wheel scrolling of the right pane, chunked terminal input parsing,\ndocs/architecture.md:284:rendering invalidation, and polling background workers. Insert mode sends chat text to the selected\ndocs/architecture.md:285:agent session, or to `command-agent` when the Work Leaf command surface is selected. Bracketed-paste\ndocs/architecture.md:286:newlines and Shift+Enter are chat prompt line breaks. A plain Enter submits the buffered chat text.\ndocs/architecture.md:287:When an agent chat is selected in command mode, `/` focuses the chat, seeds the chat buffer with\ndocs/architecture.md:288:`/`, and enters insert mode so `/status`-style input submits through the same selected-agent chat\ndocs/architecture.md:291:The terminal app maps a session to a left-pane `READY` marker when the controller exposes no loading\ndocs/architecture.md:292:state for that session. `TerminalUi` queues one terminal bell when a chat transitions into the ready\ndocs/architecture.md:293:state and renders ready rows in reverse video so they remain highlighted until the chat becomes busy\ndocs/architecture.md:296:`src/ui.rs::TerminalUi` owns terminal-specific presentation state:\ndocs/architecture.md:298:- `UiMode`, `PaneFocus`, `UiSurface`, `UiKey`, and `UiAction` model terminal interactions.\ndocs/architecture.md:299:- `AgentListEntry` is the terminal left-pane representation of an agent row.\ndocs/architecture.md:301:- `TerminalUi` renders left/right panes, prompts, cursor placement, command-interface selection, and\ndocs/architecture.md:302:  terminal navigation actions. The right pane keeps the chat prompt visible while scroll offsets\ndocs/architecture.md:305:`src/ui_harness.rs::UiHarness` is the test harness for terminal behavior. It exercises the same\ndocs/architecture.md:307:`UiHarness::handle_byte` or `UiHarness::handle_bytes` rather than duplicating terminal input logic.\ndocs/architecture.md:309:A web UI, desktop UI, or non-terminal integration should not depend on `TerminalApp` or\ndocs/architecture.md:315:agents. It uses `FileLockTable` for file reads and command write locks, `CommandWritePolicy` for\ndocs/architecture.md:316:command classification, `PatchCoordinator` for patch requests, and the active `AgentBackend` for\ndocs/architecture.md:321:heuristic read-only/write-intent classification for commands when an agent is unsure. Agent-requested\ndocs/architecture.md:322:command runs execute in the project root while `FileLockTable` holds write locks for the normalized\ndocs/architecture.md:328:command has produced the tracked working-tree change, so the command output can be saved as the\ndocs/architecture.md:333:`src/review.rs::GitHistory` reads latest agent commits from repository history, builds cumulative\ndocs/architecture.md:334:review targets for a patch agent since a launch or reviewed baseline, and resolves agent metadata\ndocs/architecture.md:335:commits by exact hash. `ReviewCoordinator<B>` launches reviewer agents against those review targets\ndocs/architecture.md:336:and loops until the reviewer reports no findings or the configured maximum round count is reached.\ndocs/architecture.md:337:`CommandChat` resolves reviewer `@work-leaf` directives, such as file reads, before interpreting\ndocs/architecture.md:338:reviewer output as findings. `CommandChat` and `WorkLeafController` keep a stable\ndocs/architecture.md:339:`review-<agent-id>` reviewer identity for each patch agent and skip latest agent heads that have\ndocs/architecture.md:340:already completed review. `AgentCommit`, `ReviewResult`, and `ReviewError` are the public review\ndocs/architecture.md:341:workflow types. `WorkLeafController` scopes automatic review after a patch agent reports done to the\ndocs/architecture.md:342:patch agent that produced the provisional commit; explicit review commands use the history-wide\ndocs/architecture.md:343:review target lookup.\ndocs/architecture.md:345:`src/linearize.rs::LinearizePlanner<B>` prepares linearization questions and launches a linearizer\ndocs/architecture.md:349:exact commits recorded as reviewed in the current command-chat or controller instance; unrelated\ndocs/architecture.md:350:historical agent metadata commits are outside the linearizer scope unless the user explicitly reviews\ndocs/architecture.md:351:or adds them in that session. When one patch-agent id completes multiple reviewed commits in one\ndocs/architecture.md:352:active instance, each reviewed hash is listed independently for the linearizer.\ndocs/architecture.md:355:for agent launch prompts.\ndocs/architecture.md:357:`src/chat_title.rs` is crate-private. It builds the prompt used for hidden chat-title backend\ndocs/architecture.md:359:first-prompt fallback, and tracks which sessions have already requested a generated title.\ndocs/architecture.md:383:6. Use `WorkLeafController` or `CommandChat` without modifying terminal UI code.\ndocs/architecture.md:393:surfaces for UIs, agent providers, command orchestration, and core workflows. In Rust, removing or\ndocs/architecture.md:400:integration, command orchestration, or core workflow integration.\ndocs/architecture.md:416:instead of duplicating internal terminal or worker logic. Terminal UI behavior is covered through\ndocs/architecture.md:417:`tests/ui_harness.rs`, `tests/terminal_ui.rs`, and `tests/terminal_app.rs`.\ndocs/architecture.md:421:covered by `tests/start_script.rs`, which runs the root `start` script through a pseudo-terminal with\ndocs/architecture.md:426:`tests/reviews.rs`, `tests/linearize.rs`, and `tests/workspace.rs` provide the current coverage\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:10:and review routing share the same coordination model. When the process is launched with\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:18:The command agent is the always-available user-facing control surface. The user interacts with it to\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:19:start work, create patch agents, ask for orchestration actions, and run high-level commands through\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:22:The command surface is represented by `src/cli.rs::CommandChat` and the controller, transport, and\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:23:terminal adapters around it:\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:25:- `src/cli.rs::CommandChat` owns the backend, file lock table, read tracker, command policy, known\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:26:  agents, and review/linearize entry points.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:27:- `src/workspace.rs::WorkLeafController` exposes the UI-neutral control surface used by the terminal\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:32:- `src/terminal_app.rs::RemoteTerminalApp` adapts terminal input and rendering to the HTTP client\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:34:- `src/terminal_app.rs::TerminalApp` adapts terminal input and rendering to an in-process\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:37:The command agent is not a patch author. Its job is to coordinate work and create the correct agent\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:42:A patch agent is created by the user, usually through the command agent, to add a feature, fix a bug,\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:69:A review agent is run by the orchestrator to review a patch agent's work. The review agent reviews\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:72:The review agent MUST review only behavior introduced or modified by the reviewed patch. It must not\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:74:reviewed patch makes them worse, depends on them, or claims to fix them.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:80:3. The orchestrator prompts the patch agent to continue when the agent has not emitted\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:81:   `@work-leaf done`.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:82:4. The patch agent runs required checks through locked command directives, submits follow-up patches\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:83:   when needed, and emits `@work-leaf done` when the patch is ready for review.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:84:5. The orchestrator runs a review agent for that patch.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:85:6. If the review agent emits `@work-leaf` directives, the orchestrator resolves those directives for\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:86:   the review agent before treating reviewer output as findings.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:87:7. If there are no findings, the user can mark the review-agent chat as done.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:89:9. The patch agent keeps patching through the orchestrator until the review agent reports no\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:92:The current implementation path for this review loop builds patch-agent review targets through\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:93:`src/review.rs::GitHistory`, asks the original agent for a summary, launches or resumes the patch\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:94:agent's `review-<agent-id>` reviewer, resolves reviewer orchestrator directives such as file reads,\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:95:sends findings back to the original agent, and asks the reviewer to recheck until `NO_FINDINGS` or\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:96:the round limit. Command-chat and controller review startup keep one reviewer identity per patch\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:97:agent and skip latest agent heads that already completed a review pass. Automatic review requires an\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:98:applied patch from the patch agent and that agent's `@work-leaf done` directive, and is scoped to all\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:99:provisional commits from that patch agent since the launch or latest reviewed baseline. An explicit\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:100:`review` command is the history-wide review entry point.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:108:Inspection agents are useful for planning, debugging, architecture review, log inspection, and\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:118:System agents are run by the orchestrator in the background for internal work such as chat naming,\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:125:- hidden `title-<agent-id>` backend launches generate chat titles from the first prompt;\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:126:  `src/chat_title.rs::ChatTitleAgent` tracks which chats have requested title generation.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:134:Agent launch prompts include a file-access policy.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:135:`src/agent.rs::PromptPolicy` injects rules selected by `src/agent.rs::ReadPermission`.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:137:With `ReadPermission::Orchestrator`, prompts tell agents:\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:143:- use `@work-leaf locks classify <command>` when the agent is unsure whether a command writes\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:145:- use `@work-leaf locks run <path> <path...> -- <command>` to run commands while the orchestrator\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:146:  holds write locks for paths the command may write;\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:147:- keep locked command runs within five minutes unless the user authorizes a longer lock-holding\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:148:  command;\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:149:- use `@work-leaf done` when no more orchestrator work is required.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:151:With `ReadPermission::DirectFilesystem`, prompts tell agents:\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:154:- use read-only inspection commands for repository context instead of `@work-leaf read`;\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:157:- use `@work-leaf locks classify <command>` when the agent is unsure whether a command writes\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:159:- use `@work-leaf locks run <path> <path...> -- <command>` to run commands while the orchestrator\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:160:  holds write locks for paths the command may write;\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:161:- keep locked command runs within five minutes unless the user authorizes a longer lock-holding\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:162:  command;\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:163:- use `@work-leaf done` when no more orchestrator work is required.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:171:2. `src/codex.rs::CodexBackend::build_launch_invocation` injects the policy into a launch prompt.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:264:before the reader submits a patch or reports done, the reader may be about to produce a stale diff.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:275:- `@work-leaf done` clears all pending read entries for that agent.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:277:After a successful patch, the orchestrator prompts the patch agent to continue when the same\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:278:directive turn does not include `@work-leaf done`:\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:284:Run any required or relevant checks through `@work-leaf locks run <path>... -- <command>` when the command may write files.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:285:Keep locked command runs within five minutes unless the user authorizes a longer lock-holding command.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:286:Provide additional patches if checks fail or more work is needed; emit `@work-leaf done` only when this patch is ready for review.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:344:protocol prompt asking the agent to resend a complete unified diff.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:348:Agents can ask whether a command is write-producing when they are unsure:\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:355:format, package, compiler, and language runtime commands. For example, `cargo test` is treated as\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:357:advice for uncertain cases; agents that know a command may write project files can skip\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:369:deduplicates the supplied paths, acquires the corresponding write locks, and runs the command in the\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:370:project root. Locked command runs have a five-minute default timeout. When a locked command exceeds\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:371:that timeout, the orchestrator terminates it, releases the locks, returns a timed-out command result to\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:372:the agent, and requires user authorization before a longer lock-holding command is run. The\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:373:orchestrator sends the command status, stdout, stderr, timeout state, and locked paths back to the\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:374:same agent as `work-leaf command result`. The command output is agent context; manual feature edits\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:377:If a locked command leaves tracked file changes under the requested lock paths, those paths are\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:378:tracked as pending command changes for that patch agent. The agent cannot finish with\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:379:`@work-leaf done` while pending command changes remain. The orchestrator returns the current tracked\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:384:The command-lock rule is language- and tool-agnostic. Agents use it for any formatter, build, test,\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:386:may write files. The agent chooses the command from repository instructions and project context, and\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:388:that command may write.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:392:A provisional patch commit records metadata that review and linearization use:\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:399:`src/patch.rs::GitPatcher::git_commit` creates this metadata commit. `src/review.rs::GitHistory`\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:402:The review flow uses that metadata to connect review findings back to the patch agent that produced\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:403:the patch. Automatic review starts only after the patch agent has an applied patch and reports\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:404:`@work-leaf done`; patch application alone is not a review-readiness signal. Each patch agent uses a\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:405:stable `review-<agent-id>` reviewer identity. The review agent must focus only on the reviewed patch.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:406:Reviewer `@work-leaf` directives are resolved in the reviewer conversation before output is\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:407:interpreted as findings. If the reviewer finds issues, the orchestrator sends those findings to the\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:409:the reviewer reports no findings, the review chat can be marked done by the user.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:417:2. The command agent is available as the control surface.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:418:3. The user asks the command agent to create a patch agent for a feature or bug fix.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:427:9. The orchestrator returns a patch-applied continuation prompt when the patch agent has not reported\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:428:   done.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:429:10. The patch agent runs required checks through locked command directives, commits or reverts any\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:430:    tracked command output through the patch protocol, and reports `@work-leaf done` when the patch is\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:431:    ready for review.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:432:11. The orchestrator runs or schedules that patch agent's review agent for the patch.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:433:12. The review agent reviews only behavior introduced or modified by the patch.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:434:13. The review agent can request file text through the orchestrator before reporting findings.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:435:14. If the review agent reports findings, the orchestrator sends them to the patch agent and the patch\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:437:15. If the review agent reports no findings, the user can mark the review chat as done.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:438:16. Reviewed work from the current command-chat or controller instance can then be linearized into the\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:442:write, review, and linearization steps remain orchestrator-controlled.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:446:The example below shows the default read-permission interaction, not the raw terminal rendering.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:448:The user asks the command agent:\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:454:The command agent creates a patch agent:\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:522:The orchestrator runs a review agent:\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:525:review-user-1: review JSON config parsing patch\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:528:If the review agent finds a regression introduced by the patch, the orchestrator sends the findings\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:532:If the review agent reports:\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:538:the user can mark the review chat as done and proceed toward linearization.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:544:- `src/agent.rs::PromptPolicy`: injects file-access rules into agent prompts.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:545:- `src/agent.rs::ReadPermission`: selects orchestrator-mediated or direct filesystem read prompts.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:548:- `src/cli.rs::CommandChat`: owns the command surface, backend, file locks, read tracker, and\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:555:- `src/orchestrator.rs::handle_agent_directives_streaming`: handles reads, patches, command\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:556:  classification, sends, done, stale updates, and follow-up routing.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:560:- `src/locks.rs::CommandWritePolicy`: classifies commands that write project files.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:563:- `src/review.rs::ReviewCoordinator`: runs reviewer conversations over agent patch commits.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:564:- `src/review.rs::GitHistory`: finds latest agent commits from git history and builds cumulative\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:565:  review targets since launch or latest reviewed baselines.\ndocs/orchestrator-file-workflow.md:567:- `src/terminal_app.rs::RemoteTerminalApp`: renders the CLI terminal UI through the HTTP controller\n","exit_code":2,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '260,460p' docs/architecture.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '260,460p' docs/architecture.md\"","aggregated_output":"- `POST /command` calls `WorkLeafController::execute_command_line`.\n- `POST /command-agent` calls `WorkLeafController::send_command_agent_message`.\n- `POST /agent/message` calls `WorkLeafController::send_message`.\n- `POST /agent/interrupt` calls `WorkLeafController::interrupt_agent`.\n- `POST /transcript` calls `WorkLeafController::push_transcript_line`.\n- `POST /loading-text` calls `WorkLeafController::loading_text`.\n- `POST /shutdown` calls `WorkLeafController::shutdown` and stops the daemon loop.\n\n`src/http_controller.rs::HttpControllerClient` is the matching blocking localhost client. It\nserializes and deserializes the same workspace DTOs used by in-process frontends. `AgentId`\ndeserialization uses `src/agent.rs::AgentId::new`, so HTTP payloads preserve the same identifier\nvalidation as local controller calls.\n\n## Terminal UI\n\nThe terminal frontend is an adapter over the UI-neutral controller surface.\n\n`src/terminal_app.rs::TerminalApp<B>` translates raw terminal bytes and modal editing state into\ndirect `WorkLeafController<B>` calls for in-process use. `src/terminal_app.rs::RemoteTerminalApp`\nuses the same terminal state machine with `HttpControllerClient` for the CLI/daemon process split.\nBoth adapters keep a local render snapshot, apply `WorkLeafEvent` values to that cache and to\n`TerminalUi`, and render from the cache rather than fetching a full controller snapshot for every\nframe. They own terminal event-loop concerns such as insert mode, prompt mode, `Ctrl-W` navigation,\nSGR mouse clicks, SGR mouse wheel scrolling of the right pane, chunked terminal input parsing,\nrendering invalidation, and polling background workers. Insert mode sends chat text to the selected\nagent session, or to `command-agent` when the Work Leaf command surface is selected. Bracketed-paste\nnewlines and Shift+Enter are chat prompt line breaks. A plain Enter submits the buffered chat text.\nWhen an agent chat is selected in command mode, `/` focuses the chat, seeds the chat buffer with\n`/`, and enters insert mode so `/status`-style input submits through the same selected-agent chat\npath.\n\nThe terminal app maps a session to a left-pane `READY` marker when the controller exposes no loading\nstate for that session. `TerminalUi` queues one terminal bell when a chat transitions into the ready\nstate and renders ready rows in reverse video so they remain highlighted until the chat becomes busy\nagain.\n\n`src/ui.rs::TerminalUi` owns terminal-specific presentation state:\n\n- `UiMode`, `PaneFocus`, `UiSurface`, `UiKey`, and `UiAction` model terminal interactions.\n- `AgentListEntry` is the terminal left-pane representation of an agent row.\n- `TerminalLayout` computes pane geometry.\n- `TerminalUi` renders left/right panes, prompts, cursor placement, command-interface selection, and\n  terminal navigation actions. The right pane keeps the chat prompt visible while scroll offsets\n  reveal earlier transcript rows.\n\n`src/ui_harness.rs::UiHarness` is the test harness for terminal behavior. It exercises the same\n`TerminalUi` frame path used by the interactive example. UI tests should drive\n`UiHarness::handle_byte` or `UiHarness::handle_bytes` rather than duplicating terminal input logic.\n\nA web UI, desktop UI, or non-terminal integration should not depend on `TerminalApp` or\n`TerminalUi`; it should depend on `WorkLeafController` and the DTOs in `src/workspace.rs`.\n\n## Core Workflow Modules\n\n`src/orchestrator.rs::AgentOrchestrator<B>` parses and executes `@work-leaf` directives emitted by\nagents. It uses `FileLockTable` for file reads and command write locks, `CommandWritePolicy` for\ncommand classification, `PatchCoordinator` for patch requests, and the active `AgentBackend` for\nrouted follow-up messages. Its public output is `OrchestratorEvent`.\n\n`src/locks.rs::FileLockTable` owns root-scoped path normalization and read/write locking.\n`FileSnapshot` carries file read results. `CommandWritePolicy` and `CommandWriteIntent` provide\nheuristic read-only/write-intent classification for commands when an agent is unsure. Agent-requested\ncommand runs execute in the project root while `FileLockTable` holds write locks for the normalized\nlock paths supplied by the agent. File paths are normalized relative to the project root and cannot\nescape that root.\n\n`src/patch.rs::GitPatcher` validates and applies unified diffs under write locks and creates\nmetadata commits for accepted patches. It also accepts a matching already-applied diff when a locked\ncommand has produced the tracked working-tree change, so the command output can be saved as the\nagent's provisional patch. `PatchCoordinator<B>` connects patch conflicts and malformed patch\ndiagnostics back to the active agent backend. `PatchRequest`, `PatchOutcome`, and `PatchError` are\nthe public patch workflow types.\n\n`src/review.rs::GitHistory` reads latest agent commits from repository history, builds cumulative\nreview targets for a patch agent since a launch or reviewed baseline, and resolves agent metadata\ncommits by exact hash. `ReviewCoordinator<B>` launches reviewer agents against those review targets\nand loops until the reviewer reports no findings or the configured maximum round count is reached.\n`CommandChat` resolves reviewer `@work-leaf` directives, such as file reads, before interpreting\nreviewer output as findings. `CommandChat` and `WorkLeafController` keep a stable\n`review-<agent-id>` reviewer identity for each patch agent and skip latest agent heads that have\nalready completed review. `AgentCommit`, `ReviewResult`, and `ReviewError` are the public review\nworkflow types. `WorkLeafController` scopes automatic review after a patch agent reports done to the\npatch agent that produced the provisional commit; explicit review commands use the history-wide\nreview target lookup.\n\n`src/linearize.rs::LinearizePlanner<B>` prepares linearization questions and launches a linearizer\nagent with decisions, groups, and required tests. `LinearizeAction`, `LinearizeGroup`,\n`LinearizePlan`, `LinearizeQuestion`, `LinearizeHandoff`, and `LinearizeError` are the public\nlinearization workflow types. `CommandChat` and `WorkLeafController` launch linearization from the\nexact commits recorded as reviewed in the current command-chat or controller instance; unrelated\nhistorical agent metadata commits are outside the linearizer scope unless the user explicitly reviews\nor adds them in that session. When one patch-agent id completes multiple reviewed commits in one\nactive instance, each reviewed hash is listed independently for the linearizer.\n\n`src/instructions.rs` is crate-private. It loads project instruction files used by `PromptPolicy`\nfor agent launch prompts.\n\n`src/chat_title.rs` is crate-private. It builds the prompt used for hidden chat-title backend\nlaunches, sanitizes title replies to lowercase hyphenated names capped at 80 characters, provides a\nfirst-prompt fallback, and tracks which sessions have already requested a generated title.\n\n## Extension Rules\n\nNew UI support follows this path:\n\n1. Construct a `CommandChat<B>` with the desired backend.\n2. Wrap it in `WorkLeafController<B>`.\n3. Render from `WorkLeafSnapshot` and `WorkLeafSession`.\n4. Drive user actions through controller methods.\n5. Consume `WorkLeafEvent` values from `drain_events`.\n\nOut-of-process UI support uses `HttpControllerClient` against a running `HttpControllerServer` and\nthe same snapshot, session, and event DTOs. The HTTP transport remains an adapter over\n`WorkLeafController`; new workflow behavior still belongs in the owning workflow or controller\nmodule.\n\nNew agent-provider support follows this path:\n\n1. Define an `AgentProfile` with `AgentKind::External`.\n2. Implement `AgentBackend` for the provider.\n3. Override streaming methods when the provider can emit real-time output.\n4. Return an `AgentShutdownHandle` when the provider owns child processes.\n5. Pass the profile through `CommandChat::with_agent_profile`.\n6. Use `WorkLeafController` or `CommandChat` without modifying terminal UI code.\n\nNew core workflow behavior belongs in the workflow module that owns the behavior. UI adapters should\nonly translate user input into controller calls and render controller snapshots. Agent providers\nshould only implement launch, send, streaming, and shutdown behavior.\n\n## API and Architecture Change Policy\n\nA breaking public API change requires human authorization before implementation. Public API includes\ntop-level re-exports in `src/lib.rs`, public items in public modules, and the documented integration\nsurfaces for UIs, agent providers, command orchestration, and core workflows. In Rust, removing or\nrenaming public items, changing public method signatures, adding required trait methods, changing\npublic enum matching behavior, changing public struct construction behavior, or changing documented\nsemantics can be breaking.\n\nA non-breaking public API extension does not require human authorization, but this document must\ndescribe the resulting public surface whenever the extension affects UI integration, agent-provider\nintegration, command orchestration, or core workflow integration.\n\nAn architecture change requires human authorization before implementation when the requested work can\nonly be completed by changing documented ownership, dependency direction, extension boundaries, or\nintegration paths. After authorization, this document must describe the resulting architecture in the\nsame patch as the code change.\n\nWhen compatibility is unclear, treat the change as breaking until the caller confirms otherwise.\n\n## Validation Expectations\n\nProvider-interface changes should have tests that prove an external provider can implement\n`AgentBackend` without depending on Codex-specific code. `tests/agent_provider_interface.rs` covers\nthat contract.\n\nController and UI behavior should use `WorkLeafController`, `TerminalApp`, and `UiHarness` tests\ninstead of duplicating internal terminal or worker logic. Terminal UI behavior is covered through\n`tests/ui_harness.rs`, `tests/terminal_ui.rs`, and `tests/terminal_app.rs`.\n\nThe CLI/daemon transport is covered by `tests/http_orchestrator.rs`, which starts the real\n`work-leaf-orchestrator` binary and drives it through `HttpControllerClient`. The release launcher is\ncovered by `tests/start_script.rs`, which runs the root `start` script through a pseudo-terminal with\nprebuilt test binaries.\n\nCore workflow changes should test the owning module and the integration path that consumes it. The\nexisting test suites under `tests/orchestrator_protocol.rs`, `tests/patching.rs`,\n`tests/reviews.rs`, `tests/linearize.rs`, and `tests/workspace.rs` provide the current coverage\nshape.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
